# A list is reordered by its drag handle

Every list whose order the user can change (the socials editor's links, the about page's rows, a group's channels, the go live page's sources, the column's lists in settings) moves by a **drag handle at the row's left end**: `RiDraggable`, size 16, the row's first item. Never up and down arrow buttons, not beside the handle either: the handle is the one way in, and the × or the row's other actions stay on the right.

Use the shared pair in `shared-components/Reorderable.tsx`, not a drag of your own:

- `useReorder({ keys, name, onMove, live?, keyedByIndex?, onPickUp?, hold? })`: `keys` in the order they are kept, `name(key)` what a screen reader calls the item, `onMove(key, to)` keeps the new place however the list already keeps its order (an atom, the draft, the api). `live` when something else must follow every step (the stream's frame, the column behind the settings modal); without it the rows move on a draft and the drop keeps it. `keyedByIndex` for a list with no ids, whose keys are `String(i)`. `hold` (ms) for a list inside something that scrolls on a phone: a touch picks the row up only after that long a still press, so a quick swipe on the handle still scrolls (the presets use 300).
- It gives `order` (draw the rows in it), `held`, `list` (spread on the rows' direct parent), `item(key)` (spread on each row), `handle(key)` (spread on the row's `DragHandle`) and `status` (the polite live region: put it beside the list, in a `relative` parent, relative-parent.mdc).
- `<DragHandle id={…} {...reorder.handle(key)} />`: a focusable `<button>`, `aria-label="move <item>"`, `aria-roledescription="drag handle"`, `touch-action: none` (a finger on it drags and does not scroll the page), `text-textTertiary hover:text-textPrimary cursor-grab active:cursor-grabbing`, its tooltip `drag to move` (tooltips.mdc).

What it does, so every list behaves the same:

- **Pointer** (mouse, pen, touch): press the handle and drag; the row follows across rows of any height; let go to drop. Escape mid-drag puts it back.
- **Keyboard**, on the focused handle: space (or enter) picks the row up, up and down arrows move it a row a press, space drops it, escape cancels and restores the order it was picked up from. Tabbing away drops it where it is.
- **Announced**: the live region says `picked up <item>, position 2 of 5`, then `<item>, position 3 of 5` at each step, then `dropped …` or `move cancelled. …`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — arrow buttons, or a pointer drag written again in the list
<button aria-label={`move ${word} up`} onClick={() => move(i, 'up')}><RiArrowUpLine size={16} /></button>
<button aria-label={`move ${word} down`} onClick={() => move(i, 'down')}><RiArrowDownLine size={16} /></button>

// ✅ GOOD
const reorder = useReorder({ keys, name: (key) => labelOf(key), onMove: (key, to) => moveItem(key, to) });
<div className="relative">
  <ul {...reorder.list}>
    {reorder.order.map((key) => (
      <li key={key} {...reorder.item(key)} className="flex items-center gap-2">
        <DragHandle id={`thing-drag-${key}`} {...reorder.handle(key)} />
        …
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  {reorder.status}
</div>
```

The about page's cells within a row (left and right, a grid) are not a vertical list and keep their move left/right buttons.
